## Purpose

Kitchen Sink handbook and showcase routes are built for **progressive enhancement**: the page story—title, lead copy, primary links, and section structure—must render from **server-generated HTML** in `<main id="main">` (or `<main>`). Scripts under `js/` (**Ksj** family: `forge-theme.js`, `portal-nav.js`, `ks-diagram-modal.js`, `forge-data-charts.js`, and related modules) may add tabs, theme switching, diagram modals, and chart bindings, but must not be the only source of critical copy.

This deterministic rule opens the same URL twice: once with crawl metrics (JS on) and once in a Playwright context with **`javaScriptEnabled: false`**. It compares visible word counts, in-main **`h1`** presence, and optional **`<noscript>`** fallback text inside `main#main` (or the first `<main>`).

**Plan:** Treat content-heavy handbook and showcase pages as targets. **Do:** Emit headlines and body in Python/React generators; gate interactive demos behind script without clearing the static story. **Check:** `metrics.jsProgressiveReport` shows sufficient no-JS retention on pages above the enabled word floor. **Adjust:** Pair with **`DET.JS.NO_CONSOLE_ERROR`** so scripted enhancements do not throw while progressive baseline passes.

## Passing signals

- **`metrics.jsProgressiveReport`**: `{ enabled, disabled }` snapshots with `disabled` non-null after `collectJsProgressiveReport` (or precomputed crawl metrics).
- On pages with **`enabled.mainWordCount` ≥ 96** (`MIN_ENABLED_MAIN_WORDS`), no-JS effective words (`max(disabled.mainWordCount, disabled.noscriptWordCount)`) stay **≥ 48** (`MIN_NOJS_MAIN_WORDS`) **or** retention **≥ 0.38** (`MIN_WORD_RETENTION_RATIO`) of enabled main words.
- **`enabled.h1Count` ≥ 1** implies **`disabled.h1Count` ≥ 1**—primary headline visible without script (no JS-only H1 injection).
- Static HTML carries the page intent: `font-display` **`h1`**, `forge-support` lead, `forge-card` / `ks-section` blocks, and handbook **`doc-main`** layout classes present before any client bundle runs.
- **`fs-tab-panel`** panes include tab labels and pane body copy in the DOM; Bootstrap tab JS only switches visibility—it does not create the only readable text.
- Charts, diagram modals, ambient motion, and theme dropdowns are enhancements; disabling script leaves a credible, readable page (not an empty shell plus a one-line `<noscript>` stub).
- Thin or utility pages below the enabled word floor are skipped (no false positives on sparse routes).
- Showcase and consumer handbook builds pass the same comparison on audited URLs.

## Failing signals

- **`critical-text-collapse`** (**major** or **critical** when retention &lt; 0.18): message *Critical main content is not available when JavaScript is disabled*; evidence includes `enabled_main_words=`, `nojs_main_words=`, `noscript_words=`, `retention=`, `min_retention=0.38`.
- **`missing-primary-heading`** (**major**): *The primary in-main headline is missing when JavaScript is disabled*; evidence `enabled_h1=… nojs_h1=0`.
- **`noscript-fallback`** (**minor**): main collapses without script and **`<noscript>`** has fewer than **12** words while no `critical-text-collapse` finding was already raised.
- Inline or boot scripts that **`innerHTML`** the entire `<main>` on load—harness defect pattern: empty static shell, 200 filler words appear only with JS.
- React or client-only render paths that omit server HTML for titles and prose on handbook routes.
- **`disabled: null`** with `skippedReason` (navigation timeout, no browser)—audit skipped for that URL; fix infrastructure before treating as pass.
- Page may pass **`DET.JS.NO_CONSOLE_ERROR`** while failing here—console hygiene does not prove noscript baseline.

## Before example

Failing KS markup: static `<main>` is nearly empty; a boot script replaces all main content when JavaScript runs (same pattern as the ruleset harness defect fixture `det-js-progressive-fail.html`).

```html
<main id="main" class="col-lg-9 col-xl-10 px-3 px-md-5 pt-4 pb-5 doc-main">
  <noscript>
    <p class="forge-support mb-0">Enable JavaScript to view this handbook page.</p>
  </noscript>
  <script>
    (function () {
      const main = document.getElementById('main');
      if (!main) return;
      main.innerHTML =
        '<h1 class="font-display mb-3">Interaction scripts</h1>' +
        '<p class="forge-support mb-3">' + 'Progressive enhancement requires static HTML. '.repeat(24) + '</p>' +
        '<section class="fs-tab-panel" hash="Ksj" data-ks-hash="Ksj" data-ks-type="script-family" data-ks-name="Kitchen Sink interaction scripts">' +
        '<div class="forge-card breathe-static p-3"><p class="forge-support mb-0">Tab body injected only after script.</p></div>' +
        '</section>';
    })();
  </script>
</main>
```

With JavaScript disabled, auditors see only the brief `<noscript>` line—far below **48** words and **38%** retention vs the JS-enabled main.

